<<189 Generation>> Why is plastic used to cover wires and not metal?

[STRDGM] The image shows a close-up of a kettle and a circuit board, highlighting the use of wires and electrical components. The kettle is plugged into a socket, illustrating the flow of electricity. [STPDGM]

\subsection*{Answers}
Plastic is used to cover wires because it is an insulator, preventing the flow of electricity to other objects or people. Metal, being a conductor, would allow electricity to flow through it, making it unsafe for covering wires. The plastic covering, or insulation, helps to keep the electricity inside the wire, ensuring safe and efficient transmission of electrical current. [STRDGM] The image shows a close-up of a kettle and a circuit board, highlighting the use of wires and electrical components. The kettle is plugged into a socket, illustrating the flow of electricity. [STPDGM] <<Generation>>
